Chromium's Linux input-method layer keeps no plain copy of what an input method types into a password field: the
text-input-v3 event strings, the Wayland context's commit and preedit conversions, InputMethodAuraLinux's composition
and pending commit, and the GTK context's commit and preedit conversions are zeroed before their memory is released or
reused. On Wayland, SurroundingTextTracker only ever sees a TEXT_INPUT_TYPE_PASSWORD client's text as a mask of the same
UTF-8 and UTF-16 lengths, so delete-surrounding and cursor offsets still resolve while its history holds no plaintext.
